  "en": {
   "title": "How Does Weather Radar See Invisible Rain in the Sky?",
   "description": "It's raining far away, hidden behind clouds, so how does a weather radar already know? This short explains the real science of weather radar: electromagnetic waves (microwave pulses), reflection and echoes, the Doppler effect and how radar measures rainfall in millimetres per hour.\n\nRadar pulses slip past tiny cloud droplets but bounce off bigger raindrops. The echo's travel time gives the distance, and its strength shows how heavy the rain is. A Doppler weather radar also measures how fast precipitation is moving toward or away from it, and drops rushing in on one side and away on the other reveal a rotating storm, the kind of pattern behind tornado warnings. That is how forecasters see rain before it reaches you.\n\nSubscribe to Merit Mantra for more conceptual clarity, and comment the next science question you want answered.",
